  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An isolated shower or thunderstorm will be possible \nthis afternoon, mainly well inland from the lakes. Additional \nchances for showers will come tonight through Wednesday. \n\nHigh pressure extending in across the eastern Great Lakes will \nweaken and then exit off to our east today. Meanwhile...a compact \nshortwave dropping southeast through the broader longwave trough \nwill track east into the central Great Lakes by late this afternoon-\neve. While a vast majority of the region will continue to see dry \nweather there will be the potential for an isolated shower or \nthunderstorm during peak heating. Low chances (< 20%) given weak \nforcing and limited moisture ( PW < 1.0 inch) but we still can't \nrule out seeing something fire up well inland from the lakes. \nAdditionally...winds pick up a bit northeast of the lakes this \nafternoon as a modest jet aligns with the lakes with gusts up to 25 \nmph. The compact shortwave will near the Lower Lakes late tonight \nand then pass by to our north on Wednesday while weakening as it \nheads off into Quebec. As it passes through the area we will see a \nslightly better chance of showers and even the potential for a few \nrumbles of thunder.   \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Anomalously strong low pressure system will bring \nincreasing chances for showers and thunderstorms Wednesday eve-night \ninto Thursday, along with the potential for strong gusty winds.  \n\nA strong mid-level shortwave trough ejects out of the upper Mid-west \nWednesday and then races east. Meanwhile...a rapidly deepening sfc \nlow will begin to track through Michigan by Wednesday eve and then \nslowly to our west overnight. While the details will still need to \nbe worked out...there is the potential for strong gusty winds \nWednesday night through the day Thursday, and also widespread \nshowers and possible some strong to severe storms. Lets talk \nwinds...an anomalously strong LLJ (+60-70 knots) will come racing \ninto WNY late Wednesday eve-night. There is the potential we may see \nsome strong gusty winds west of the Chautauqua ridge, and also \nacross the higher terrain. That said...winds may 'not' materialize \n(low confidence) given guidance shows precip rapidly moving in with \nthe LLJ. Even so...this threat still needs to be mention in the HWO \nwith potential future headlines. With the passage of the low just to \nour west there will be another opportunity to see strong gusty winds \non Thursday. Although weakening...a fairly decent LLJ will still \nreside across the region Thursday. Given decent isallobaric pressure \nrises with the passage of the low and steepening lapse rates we \ncould see winds gusting up to 45 mph or greater. This will \nespecially be the case northeast of both lakes Thursday afternoon \ninto the evening hours. Backing up a bit...the overall evolution of \nconvection Wednesday night will need to be monitor closely too as \nthe environment will strong to severe storms. Although the \ntiming is less than favorable with a more likely scenario of a \nswath of moderate to heavy rain with embedded thunder moving \nthrough the region. Last but not least...PW values will be found\nin excess of 1.5 inches with any of the stronger embedded \nconvection there is the potential for heavy rainfall.",
